// Chill-Box security rules. Deploy them, do not paste them:
//
//   npx -y firebase-tools deploy --only database --project chill-box-e5d6b
//
// firebase.json at the repo root points at this file. Hand-pasting is how
// the published rules came to sit a commit behind the client, refusing a
// field the client had started writing and bricking registration while the
// app blamed the network. See MEMORY.md §16.
//
// No skipper ever logs in - the app signs each phone in anonymously behind
// the scenes - so these rules are the only thing standing between a public
// web config and the harbour's data.
//
// WHAT THESE RULES ENFORCE
//
//   * every write needs a signed-in identity. The app signs each phone in
//     anonymously, so nothing changes for the skipper, but the public web
//     config on its own no longer grants anyone write access
//   * anyone may read - the boxes are public information, that is the point
//   * A CRATE BELONGS TO A BOAT, AND A BOAT BELONGS TO A PHONE. Write
//     permission is granted on the individual slot, never on the node above
//     it, so the rule can ask the only question that matters: is this your
//     boat? You cannot claim a slot for a boat that is not yours, and you
//     cannot move a crate held by someone else's boat
//   * because permission is per slot, there is no write anywhere that
//     touches more than one crate without being checked against that crate's
//     owner. A client cannot delete or overwrite a slot held by a boat that
//     belongs to another phone — one write at a time or in bulk
//
// THE LIMIT OF THAT, STATED HERE AND NOT THIRTY LINES DOWN: the check is
// per crate, so it protects a crate whose boat someone has CLAIMED. A boat
// no device has claimed is open to anyone, and in the shipped demo roster
// no boat is claimed — so a signed-in client can still empty a harbour of
// unclaimed boats, one slot at a time. That is the price of a demo that
// works from a cold start, and it shrinks to nothing as skippers sign in.
//   * a boat's binding to a phone (`uid`) is set once and can never be
//     reassigned, dropped, or set to anyone else's identity
//   * a slot must always be a well-formed slot: a known status, a box name
//     and slot index in range, and a boat that actually exists here
//   * only the last four digits of a mobile number are ever stored, and they
//     cannot be rewritten once set. No boat can be deleted, because slots
//     point at boats
//   * a ledger row, once written, can never be edited or deleted
//
// BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT THIS IS NOT. These rules check the shape of the data,
// the writer's identity, and who owns a crate. They are not harbour policy.
// A signed-in client that ignores the app can still:
//
//   * exceed the 2-crate cap, which no rule can count across three boxes
//   * append invented ledger rows. Rows cannot be edited, but the report can
//     be padded by addition, which for a society billing off the CSV is the
//     same problem
//   * rewrite another boat's name or owner
//   * move a crate the harbour has given up on - a hold that ran out, or a
//     stored crate past its overstay hour. That one is deliberate: it is how
//     a space is reclaimed with no admin account, and it is a community rule
//     rather than a hole. See the slot rule below
//
// WHAT THESE RULES USED TO ALLOW, AND NO LONGER DO: any signed-in phone
// could write any boat's `status` - approving itself past the admin queue,
// or setting all twenty boats to 'blocked' and stopping the harbour from
// booking at all. That was named here as the biggest thing they did not
// stop, and no rule could fix it while the app still had approvals: with no
// admin identity, whatever the client may write, anyone may write. Deleting
// the feature is what closed it. There is no approval and no blocking, so
// `status` may only ever be the one value that has always meant "an ordinary
// boat", and the roster is not a lever any more.
//
// A BOAT NOBODY HAS CLAIMED IS OPEN TO ANYONE. That is the seeded demo
// roster, and it is what keeps Publish harbour, Reset demo and a cold-start
// demo working. The moment a skipper claims a boat, only their phone can
// move its crates.
//
// AND THE COST OF THAT BINDING: it can never be reassigned. A skipper who
// clears their browser, reinstalls, or is evicted by Android storage
// pressure gets a new anonymous identity and can never sign in as their own
// boat again, on any device. There is no admin control that can undo it. See
// README, 'What this cannot do yet'.
//
// The last four digits are world-readable, so they identify a boat; they do
// not authenticate one.
{
  "rules": {
    ".read": true,
    ".write": false,

    "harbours": {
      "$harbour": {
        "boxes": {
          ".read": true,
          // NO write permission here, deliberately. It is granted one level
          // down, on the slot — which is the whole point. A rule that can
          // write this node can write every slot in it, and cannot tell your
          // crate from mine.

          "$box": {
            "$slot": {
              // Who may move this crate. Three questions:
              //
              //   1. any boat I name in the new slot must be mine
              //   2. any boat already in the slot must be mine, unless
              //   3. the harbour has given up on it — a hold that ran out,
              //      or a stored crate past its overstay hour
              //
              // "Mine" means the boat carries this device's uid. A boat that
              // no device has claimed is open to anyone.
              //
              // Clause 3 is DERIVED FROM `depositedAt`, not from a stored
              // 'overstay' status. The overstay flag is raised by applyTick
              // on each phone's own copy and is never written to the shared
              // one, so a rule that waited for it would never fire: force
              // release was refused for every boat a skipper had claimed,
              // and the crate — with a catch rotting in it — could then be
              // cleared by nobody in the harbour, the harbour master
              // included. Every rehearsal passed because every seeded boat
              // is unbound. One rule, one source of truth: the timestamp.
              //
              // The stored 'overstay' status is still honoured because the
              // seeded demo harbour ships one.
              //
              // 14400000 is HOLD_MS and 21600000 is OVERSTAY_MS, both from
              // src/lib/time.ts. They must match.
              ".write": "auth != null && $box.matches(/^box[1-3]$/) && $slot.matches(/^[0-9]$/) && (!newData.exists() || (newData.hasChild('status') && (newData.child('status').val() === 'empty' || newData.hasChild('boatId')) && (newData.child('status').val() !== 'occupied' || newData.hasChild('depositedAt')) && (newData.child('status').val() !== 'reserved' || newData.hasChild('reservedAt')))) && (!newData.exists() || !newData.hasChild('boatId') || !root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child(newData.child('boatId').val()).child('uid').exists() || root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child(newData.child('boatId').val()).child('uid').val() === auth.uid) && (!data.exists() || !data.hasChild('boatId') || !root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child(data.child('boatId').val()).child('uid').exists() || root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child(data.child('boatId').val()).child('uid').val() === auth.uid || data.child('status').val() === 'overstay' || (data.child('status').val() === 'reserved' && data.hasChild('reservedAt') && data.child('reservedAt').val() + 14400000 <= now) || (data.child('status').val() === 'occupied' && data.hasChild('depositedAt') && data.child('depositedAt').val() + 21600000 <= now))",
              // A typo, or a client inventing 'box9' or slot 47, is refused
              // rather than quietly stored — and so is a crate with no boat
              // on it, which the reader skips and the console cannot show,
              // leaving a slot permanently full and invisible.
              //
              // A crate must also keep the timestamp its deadline is
              // measured from — `depositedAt` when stored, `reservedAt`
              // while held. Erasing one left a crate no clock could ever
              // reach: nothing could flag it overdue, and clause 3 below
              // could never let anyone else clear it. A crate abandoned by
              // its owner, unclearable by the whole harbour, one of thirty
              // gone for the life of the deployment.
              //
              // BOTH statuses, deliberately. The first fix covered
              // `occupied` and left the identical construction on
              // `reserved`, where a hold with no `reservedAt` shows a
              // frozen 4:00:00 that never counts down and never expires.
              //
              // Erasing `status` is refused for its own reason: the reader
              // defaults a missing status to `empty`, so the crate would
              // read as free to everyone while the fish were still in it.
              //
              // All three checks live in the .write above, not only here.
              // Firebase evaluates validate rules on the node written and
              // its children, NEVER on its ancestors, so writing
              // `boxes/box1/47/status` directly would skip a .validate at
              // this level entirely. A .write, by contrast, is evaluated at
              // every ancestor of the write. The duplication below is for a
              // whole-slot write and is deliberate.
              ".validate": "$slot.matches(/^[0-9]$/) && newData.hasChildren(['status'])",
              "status": {
                ".validate": "newData.isString() && (newData.val() === 'empty' || newData.val() === 'reserved' || newData.val() === 'occupied' || newData.val() === 'overstay')"
              },
              "boatId": {
                ".validate": "newData.val() === null || root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child(newData.val()).exists()"
              },
              "species": { ".validate": "newData.val() === null || newData.isString()" },
              "reservedAt": { ".validate": "newData.val() === null || newData.isNumber()" },
              "depositedAt": { ".validate": "newData.val() === null || newData.isNumber()" },
              "plannedOutAt": { ".validate": "newData.val() === null || newData.isNumber()" },
              "$other": { ".validate": false }
            }
          }
        },

        // Who the harbour has vouched for, one node per voter.
        //
        // A vouch can only ever RAISE a boat's allowance, from one crate to
        // two. There is deliberately no "no" and no way to remove a boat:
        // this app has no authority in it, and a control that could lower
        // somebody's allowance would be one. The worst a hostile phone can do
        // is vouch for a boat that did not deserve it.
        //
        // Permission is per voter, exactly like a crate slot: you may write
        // the node keyed to the boat YOUR phone holds, and nobody else's. So
        // one boat is one vouch, enforced by the database rather than by the
        // screen.
        "vouches": {
          ".read": true,
          "$applicant": {
            "$voter": {
              ".write": "auth != null && newData.val() === true && $voter !== $applicant && root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child($applicant).exists() && root.child('harbours').child($harbour).child('boats').child($voter).child('uid').val() === auth.uid",
              ".validate": "newData.val() === true"
            }
          }
        },

        "boats": {
          ".read": true,
          "$boat": {
            // Never deletable: slots point at boats, and a deleted boat would
            // orphan whatever it is holding. Rejection is a local matter.
            //
            // And a write may never drop or change an existing `uid`. Without
            // that clause, replacing the boat object without a uid would
            // silently unclaim it — and the finder could then claim it and
            // move that skipper's crates.
            // Everything a boat must always have, and everything that can
            // never change, is asserted HERE rather than in .validate below.
            // Two Firebase behaviours defeat a .validate for this job, and
            // this file already relies on both elsewhere: a .validate is
            // skipped when the value written is null, and it never runs on
            // an ancestor of the written path. So `update(boats/04,
            // { mobileLast4: null })` slipped past the immutability rule and
            // past hasChildren, erasing the digits that prove a boat is
            // yours — the boat then vanished from every roster that had not
            // already seen it. A .write, by contrast, IS evaluated at every
            // ancestor, and its `newData` is the boat as it would be after
            // the write. One place, both directions.
            // `status` is NOT in either list any more. The app has no boat
            // status: no approval, no blocking, nobody in charge. Requiring
            // it would refuse every write from the current client; refusing
            // it would refuse every write from a bundle still cached on a
            // phone. So it is optional here and pinned to 'active' below —
            // which is what closes the biggest hole these rules used to
            // document about themselves.
            ".write": "auth != null && newData.exists() && newData.hasChildren(['name', 'owner', 'mobileLast4', 'registeredAt']) && (!data.hasChild('uid') || newData.child('uid').val() === data.child('uid').val()) && (!data.hasChild('mobileLast4') || newData.child('mobileLast4').val() === data.child('mobileLast4').val())",
            ".validate": "newData.hasChildren(['name', 'owner', 'mobileLast4', 'registeredAt'])",
            "name": { ".validate": "newData.isString() && newData.val().length >= 2 && newData.val().length <= 40" },
            "nameTe": { ".validate": "newData.isString() && newData.val().length >= 1 && newData.val().length <= 40" },
            "owner": { ".validate": "newData.isString() && newData.val().length >= 2 && newData.val().length <= 60" },
            // Four digits, never the whole number: this node is world-readable
            // so any phone can name the boat holding a crate, and publishing
            // twenty fishermen's mobile numbers is a breach with no upside.
            // Immutable, because those digits are what proves you own a boat.
            "mobileLast4": { ".validate": "newData.isString() && newData.val().matches(/^[0-9]{4}$/) && (!data.exists() || newData.val() === data.val())" },
            // 'active' or nothing, and this is the whole of what used to be
            // "THE BIGGEST THING THESE RULES DO NOT STOP".
            //
            // The field used to accept 'pending', 'active' or 'blocked' from
            // any signed-in phone, because there is no admin identity to
            // check against — so anyone with the public web config could set
            // all twenty boats to 'blocked' and stop the harbour booking, or
            // approve themselves. The rule could not be tightened while the
            // app still had approvals: whatever value the client is allowed
            // to write, it is allowed to write.
            //
            // Deleting the FEATURE is what let the rule close. There is
            // nothing to block a boat with, so nothing may be written but the
            // one value that has always meant "an ordinary boat".
            "status": { ".validate": "newData.val() === 'active'" },
            // Not immutable, deliberately: the seeded roster gets its dates
            // from whichever phone seeded it, so pinning them here would make
            // a second phone's publish fail on every boat.
            "registeredAt": { ".validate": "newData.isNumber()" },
            // The device that holds this boat. First claim wins and it can
            // never be reassigned — this is the only thing that makes the
            // slot rules above mean anything, so it must not be something a
            // second phone can simply overwrite. It can only ever be your own
            // uid, so nobody can hand a boat to someone else either.
            //
            // OPTIONAL, deliberately. A client running against an older
            // published copy of these rules registers boats without it, and
            // the harbour then behaves exactly as it did before binding
            // existed. A rules deployment lagging a code deployment must
            // never lock a skipper out of registering.
            "uid": {
              ".validate": "newData.isString() && (newData.val() === auth.uid || newData.val() === data.val())"
            },
            "$other": { ".validate": false }
          }
        },

        // Append-only: each row is writable once, never edited or deleted.
        "ledger": {
          ".read": true,
          // Phones follow the newest rows by release time. Without this index
          // Firebase would sort them on the client, which means downloading
          // the whole history to every phone on the 2G target.
          ".indexOn": ["releasedAt"],
          "$entry": {
            ".write": "auth != null && !data.exists() && newData.exists()",
            ".validate": "newData.hasChildren(['boatId', 'boxId', 'crates', 'depositedAt', 'releasedAt'])",
            "boatId": { ".validate": "newData.isString()" },
            "boxId": { ".validate": "newData.isString()" },
            "crates": { ".validate": "newData.isNumber() && newData.val() >= 1 && newData.val() <= 2" },
            "species": { ".validate": "newData.val() === null || newData.isString()" },
            "depositedAt": { ".validate": "newData.isNumber()" },
            "releasedAt": { ".validate": "newData.isNumber()" },
            "overstay": { ".validate": "newData.isBoolean()" },
            // Whether the harbour took this space back at the eight-hour
            // line rather than the boat collecting. It cannot be derived
            // from the two timestamps — a skipper who collects at 8 h 01 m
            // writes an identical row — and the Harbour page must not say
            // "not collected" over a boat that turned up. Optional: rows
            // written before the rule existed have no such field.
            "reclaimed": { ".validate": "newData.isBoolean()" },
            "$other": { ".validate": false }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
